The European Parliament's report on the impact of Russian aggression on the EU's common security and defense policy, which is set to be approved on a Wednesday, recommends that EU leaders grant Ukraine the status of a candidate member. This was announced by David McAllister, the head of the European Parliament's committee on foreign affairs, as reported by "". McAllister stated, "We recommend providing Ukraine with weapons and all necessary equipment according to the needs expressed by the Ukrainian authorities, and granting the country the status of a candidate country as a clear political signal of solidarity with the brave people of Ukraine." According to McAllister, the report's authors also recommend ensuring comprehensive monitoring of EU sanctions to "strengthen the European Commission's implementation capacity for restrictive measures." The European Parliament reminded that the institution had previously supported increasing the effectiveness of the EU's common security and defense policy. This has become even more crucial in light of Russia's aggression against Ukraine. The report proposes several measures, including:

- Rapid implementation of the EU's Strategic Compass, particularly in terms of developing the operational capabilities of EU rapid response forces;
- Review and adaptation of EU missions and operations within the Common Security and Defense Policy, with a focus on providing adequate support to the governments of Ukraine and Moldova;
- Expediting the completion of the third EU-NATO joint declaration;
